# Normalize line endings so the working tree matches the index on every platform.
#
# Without this, behavior depends entirely on each developer's local
# `core.autocrlf`. On Windows (`core.autocrlf=true`) the checkout rewrites every
# tracked text file to CRLF, which causes two concrete problems:
#
#   1. `npm run format:check` (prettier 3, default `endOfLine: "lf"`) fails on
#      every dashboard file, because prettier emits LF and compares against a
#      CRLF file. `make lint-frontend` is therefore red on Windows while CI --
#      which only runs the backend `make lint` -- stays green, so the breakage
#      is invisible until someone works on a Windows checkout.
#   2. All 43 tracked `*.sh` scripts get CRLF line endings, so their shebang and
#      every line carry a trailing `\r`. bash then fails them with
#      `command not found` / `bad interpreter` on Windows checkouts.
#
# `text=auto` leaves the genuinely binary files (png, ttf, webm, webp -- git
# already reports them as `-text`) untouched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the exception: LF breaks `goto` and label matching.
*.bat text eol=crlf
